A buffer, an open file, and a half-written index all have lifetimes, but only the buffer's storage is necessarily a memory-management problem. A garbage collector can determine that a buffer is no longer reachable. It does not decide that an index must be rolled back before a lock is dropped. Compare C, C++, and D by asking where each cleanup rule is expressed and what guarantees that it runs at the required point.

\section{Make Ownership Visible at the Boundary}

In \emph{UNIX Time-Sharing System: The C Programming Language}, Dennis Ritchie, Stephen Johnson, Michael Lesk, and Brian Kernighan distinguish stack-like storage for automatic variables from more flexible allocation through library routines. The end of a pointer variable's lifetime does not release the allocation it points to. A C API must specify whether it borrows a pointer, returns a newly owned object, or consumes ownership, and which operation releases the result. Even a handle represented by an integer may require cleanup. Do not infer ownership from its machine representation.

Bjarne Stroustrup's \emph{Evolving a Language in and for the Real World: C++ 1991--2006} situates exception handling and resource-management techniques among the pressures on a mature systems language. In C++, the usual RAII design represents ownership with an object that releases its resource in its destructor. With an automatic owner, that destructor runs on ordinary scope exit and during exception unwinding, assuming normal unwinding takes place. If acquisition has multiple steps inside a constructor, give each acquired resource its own completed member or local owner before attempting the next step: a failing constructor does not run the complete object's destructor, but already constructed subobjects are cleaned up.

Andrei Alexandrescu's \emph{The D Programming Language} describes the coexistence of garbage-collected allocations and scope guards. The \emph{D Programming Language Specification}, under ``Scope Guard Statement,'' distinguishes \texttt{scope(exit)}, \texttt{scope(failure)}, and \texttt{scope(success)}. The rules distinguish normal scope exit from exception unwinding; returning a status value that denotes failure is still normal exit. This matters when a D wrapper calls a C function returning error codes.

\section{Worked Example: A Disposable Draft}

Consider generating an index in a draft file and then publishing it. Outside failures such as process termination are excluded; a failed publish leaves the draft in place and never marks it published. Require the writer to flush and successfully close before publication. The expected sequence after a write error is to close the open writer and then discard the draft. After successful publication the draft name has been consumed, so cleanup must not attempt to remove the published index.

In C, record the draft's live state and the writer's validity explicitly. Route errors through cleanup, clearing the writer flag when a close consumes the handle even if close reports an error (assuming that is the close API's contract). In C++, let the draft owner delete an uncommitted file and let a writer owner close an open handle. Construct the draft owner first, so the writer is destroyed first during unwinding. Perform and check a consuming close explicitly on the success path; do not rely on a destructor to report a failed flush. In D, register a draft-removal \texttt{scope(exit)} before registering the close guard, making both conditional on the corresponding live state. This preserves the order even on a normal error return. A \texttt{scope(failure)} for draft removal alone would miss that return.

\section{Track Obligations Through Lowering}

The invariant on every returning or unwinding edge is that an owned, untransferred handle is released once, and no cleanup edge releases a handle that another owner has taken over. For a typical function, inspect the lowered control flow: a call that unwinds before successful acquisition must bypass the release block; one after acquisition must reach it. In a test harness replace acquisitions, writes, close, and publish with stubs and inject failure at each step. Assert an event trace, not just a final file count. A double close can go unnoticed when nobody reuses the handle before the second close.

Do not propagate language-specific exceptions through a foreign interface unless its contract explicitly supports it. Translate failures at the boundary, pair allocations with the correct deallocator, and pin the calling convention separately from the ownership policy. The \href{dossier-on-modern-cc-design/chapters/17-memory-as-an-explicit-ir.md}{dossier's Memory as an Explicit IR chapter} explores representing memory operations directly in an IR. Resource obligations need similar treatment when the resource is not memory: a close, a rollback, and a move of ownership cannot all be modeled as a deallocation.

